A.push(note('*Note.* Intervals resample wordings. Parse failures in the three conditions: 1, 8, and 3 of 1,440.'));
PA('The Study 4 design gave a similar picture (Tables F2 and F3). With the target left out of the facts line, the neutral and loss frames almost never skipped (0.2% each), the gain frame, which states the target in the headline, skipped in 21.0%, and adding the target headline to the neutral and loss wordings raised skipping by 6.9 points [+0.2, +18.1] and 6.0 points [+1.5, +12.7]. Loss text without a target statement did not differ from neutral text (+0.0 [-0.6, +0.6]). With the target in the facts line, loss text lowered skipping relative to neutral text (-15.4 [-29.4, -4.0]) and the target headline raised it for the loss wording (+9.6 [+1.0, +21.3]) but not for the neutral wording (+0.4 [-10.2, +11.9]); the interaction (+9.2 [-4.2, +21.9]) was not distinguishable from zero. The registered contrast, loss minus gain, was negative in both runs (-29.2 [-44.2, -14.6] and -20.8 [-35.4, -8.3]). Without the target in the facts line this model chose hold in 61% of first choices, against 10% when the target was stated, which is consistent with an operator that has no target to reach and therefore waits.');
A.push(caption('**Table F2.** Study 6: Gemini 2.5 Flash-Lite, skip rate by frame in the Study 4 design (mean over 16 wordings, three turns left).'));
